+# Project README — Git workflow & tiny CI
+ 
+This short README documents the recommended Git workflow for this project, how to apply patches, and includes a minimal GitHub Actions CI job that runs TypeScript checks and installs dependencies on pull requests.
+
+> Keep changes small, commit often, and use feature branches + PRs. Commit messages should be short and descriptive.
+
+---
+
+## Quick local workflow (recommended)
+
+1. Create a feature branch:
+
+```bash
+git checkout -b feat/short-description
+```
+
+2. Make small focused edits; review status:
+
+```bash
+git status
+git diff            # unstaged changes
+git add <files>
+git diff --staged   # staged changes
+```
+
+3. Commit with a clear message:
+
+```bash
+git commit -m "fix(chapter): fetch metadata from /_functions/chapter and defer verses load"
+```
+
+4. Push the branch and open a Pull Request:
+
+```bash
+git push -u origin feat/short-description
+# open a PR in your Git hosting UI (GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket)
+```
+
+5. After PR review and CI green, merge to `main` (or your protected branch).
+
+---
+
+## Working with patches (when you need to send changes outside of pushing to remote)
+
+### Create a unified-diff patch from working tree
+
+```bash
+# for all unstaged changes
+git diff > my-change.patch
+
+# for staged changes
+git diff --staged > my-staged.patch
+
+# for differences between two commits
+git diff <commitA> <commitB> > changes.patch
+```
+
+### Apply a patch
+
+```bash
+# check the patch will apply cleanly
+git apply --check my-change.patch
+
+# actually apply
+git apply my-change.patch
+
+# optionally stage the changes for commit
+git apply --index my-change.patch
+```
+
+### Create a patch that preserves commit metadata (recommended when sharing commits)
+
+```bash
+# create commits first, then export them as patch files
+git format-patch origin/main..HEAD -o patches/
+
+# apply elsewhere (preserves author/date/message)
+git am patches/0001-*.patch
+```
+
+---
+
+## Recovering / inspecting history
+
+- Use `git log --oneline --graph` to inspect recent history.
+- If a full-file replacement accidentally removes important changes, you can find the earlier commit and cherry-pick or revert it.
+- `git reflog` helps recover lost state on a local machine.
+
+---
+
+## Minimal GitHub Actions CI example
+
+Create a file `.github/workflows/ci.yml` with the following contents. It runs on PRs and checks out the code, installs dependencies, and runs a TypeScript check (or an `npm test` script if you have one).
+
+```yaml
+name: CI
+
+on:
+  pull_request:
+    branches: [ main ]
+
+jobs:
+  build:
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+
+    steps:
+      - name: Checkout
+        uses: actions/checkout@v4
+
+      - name: Use Node.js 18
+        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
+        with:
+          node-version: '18'
+
+      - name: Install dependencies
+        run: npm ci
+
+      - name: Type check
+        run: npm run type-check
+        # If you don't have a type-check script, create one in package.json:
+        # "type-check": "tsc --noEmit"
+
+      - name: Run tests (optional)
+        run: npm test
+```
+
+This CI keeps the repo healthy and gives quick feedback on PRs.
+
+---
+
+## Small best-practices & tips
+
+- Protect `main` and require PR reviews + passing CI before merge.
+- Keep commits atomic (one change per commit) and write clear commit messages.
+- Use `git stash` if you need to switch branches but preserve WIP.
+- Use `URLSearchParams` in code when building query strings to avoid `?`/`&` bugs.
+- When I provide full-file replacements, commit them on a feature branch (with a message noting the replacement) so history is preserved.
+
